1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,246
This is the proposed twelve stack magnetic circuit, solved in three dimensions.

2
00:00:05,246 --> 00:00:09,724
The model includes the actual revised steel yoke and its clearance holes.

3
00:00:09,724 --> 00:00:13,282
The centre and outer magnets have opposite axial polarity.

4
00:00:13,282 --> 00:00:16,778
The coloured curves follow the calculated magnetic field.

5
00:00:16,778 --> 00:00:20,581
Moving markers indicate field direction, not plasma particles.

6
00:00:20,581 --> 00:00:22,237
The field itself is static.

7
00:00:22,237 --> 00:00:28,617
Copper, water and the nonmagnetic target are treated as having approximately unit magnetic permeability.

8
00:00:30,317 --> 00:00:37,344
This map shows the component of magnetic field parallel to the target, a quarter of a millimetre above its front face.

9
00:00:37,344 --> 00:00:41,216
The orange contour marks where the normal component crosses zero.

10
00:00:41,216 --> 00:00:45,444
It is a candidate magnetic racetrack, not a calculated erosion profile.

11
00:00:45,444 --> 00:00:52,829
Along that contour, the parallel field ranges from about eighty nine to one hundred milli tesla, with a mean of ninety five.

12
00:00:52,829 --> 00:00:55,450
The contour is continuous around the target.

13
00:00:57,150 --> 00:01:01,452
The section view shows the field arching between the centre and outer poles.

14
00:01:01,452 --> 00:01:05,074
Near the racetrack it runs approximately parallel to the target.

15
00:01:05,074 --> 00:01:11,073
This is the geometry needed to bend electron trajectories and increase their path length near the cathode.

16
00:01:11,073 --> 00:01:17,752
It does not calculate how many electrons are trapped, their residence time, or whether a particular discharge ignites.

17
00:01:17,752 --> 00:01:22,450
Those depend on the sheath electric field, gas collisions and operating conditions.

18
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:31,506
The candidate racetrack has a mean radius of about twenty millimetres, leaving approximately five millimetres to the target edge.

19
00:01:31,506 --> 00:01:36,923
The field variation around it is about ten point six percent peak to peak relative to its mean.

20
00:01:36,923 --> 00:01:39,774
That is not a ten percent coating thickness error.

21
00:01:39,774 --> 00:01:41,770
It is a magnetic uniformity metric.

22
00:01:41,770 --> 00:01:46,617
Target erosion and deposition uniformity require additional modelling or measurement.

23
00:01:48,317 --> 00:01:56,868
At the weakest point on the ring, an electron with ten electron volts of perpendicular energy has a gyroradius of about zero point one two millimetres.

24
00:01:56,868 --> 00:02:01,229
At one hundred electron volts it is about zero point three eight millimetres.

25
00:02:01,229 --> 00:02:07,911
These are small compared with the millimetre scale magnetic structure, supporting strong local electron magnetization.

26
00:02:07,911 --> 00:02:11,479
The height scan shows the field weakening away from the target.

27
00:02:11,479 --> 00:02:15,783
A small gyroradius alone does not prevent electron losses along field lines.

28
00:02:17,483 --> 00:02:23,926
For the modelled two millimetre target, the pole tips are four point five millimetres behind the sputtering surface.

29
00:02:23,926 --> 00:02:26,648
The calculated field is not weak at that spacing.

30
00:02:26,648 --> 00:02:37,646
Increasing the nonmagnetic target thickness to five millimetres increases the distance to seven point five millimetres, and the predicted mean racetrack field is still about seventy one milli tesla.

31
00:02:37,646 --> 00:02:42,700
This sensitivity result does not apply to ferromagnetic targets, which can shunt the field.

32
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:52,191
The magnetic strength screen is positive: the copper and target spacing does not appear to leave this design underpowered magnetically.

33
00:02:52,191 --> 00:02:56,750
Refining the mesh changed the mean ring field by about zero point four percent.

34
00:02:56,750 --> 00:03:02,117
An independent free space analytical check differed by about two point three percent overall.

35
00:03:02,117 --> 00:03:09,735
Ninety five milli tesla is stronger than the commonly cited twenty to fifty milli tesla range; stronger is not automatically better.

36
00:03:09,735 --> 00:03:15,564
The design still needs field mapping, operating tests, and a separate assessment of magnetic balance.

37
00:03:15,564 --> 00:03:19,950
This is a magnetostatic simulation, not a self consistent plasma simulation.
